---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { artworks } from '../lib/catalog';
import { abstractArtworks } from '../lib/abstract';
import { onchainArtworks, onchainReady } from '../lib/onchain';
const inks = artworks.filter((work) => work.artistName === 'nnxyz').length;
const scrimshaw = artworks.length - inks;
const later = onchainReady ? onchainArtworks.length : 0;
const nnxyzTotal = inks + abstractArtworks.length + later;
---

<Layout title="About the collection — The nnxyz Rooms">
  <article class="text-page">
    <h1>About the collection</h1>
    <p>
      The collector’s own introduction is on the front page. Everything else on
      the site, the wing and room texts, the artwork labels and the story pages,
      is unsigned gallery text written from the works, their archived records,
      the public ledgers and the artist’s own posts.
    </p>
    <h2>The artwork and the archive</h2>
    <p>
      This gallery brings together {nnxyzTotal} nnxyz works and {scrimshaw}{' '}
      works by Scrimshaw. The Nifty Ink archive preserves {artworks.length}{' '}
      drawings representing 633 owned editions at its saved snapshots.
      {abstractArtworks.length} Abstract works have been recovered from the
      collector’s earlier public gallery
      {later > 0 && (
        <>
          , and {later} later works on Polygon, Ethereum, Optimism, Arbitrum and
          Tezos are shown from their original files
        </>
      )}
      .
    </p>
    <p>
      Each Nifty Ink drawing is reconstructed as a native vector image from its
      archived brush paths. You can enlarge it, download its SVG, or watch the
      drawing emerge. The paths and their order are recorded; playback timing is
      reconstructed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Abstract is presented from its original published JPEGs, with enlargement
      and downloads. Its earlier gallery records fifty numbered pieces, an
      origin titled #0000 and a proof-of-stake work titled #pos. The recovered
      records do not include brush paths or establish current token ownership.
    </p>
    <p>
      The artwork pages link to the collected NFTs. Nifty Ink links use the
      editions recorded in the archived collection; Abstract links come from the
      collector’s earlier gallery; the later works link to their chain’s
      explorer or to objkt.
    </p>
    <h2>Collection history</h2>
    <p>
      NNOTB means “New Nifty on the Block,” as recorded in the collector’s
      <a href="https://www.thingelstad.com/art/nnxyz/">earlier gallery</a>. That
      account describes Faces as the first Nifty Ink series and Rawblocks as the
      third. Each collection keeps its original artwork titles; an “origin”
      title does not necessarily mark the first recorded drawing.
    </p>
    <p>
      The story of the collection, from the drawing board to the handovers of
      the complete sets, is told on <a href="/story/">the story pages</a>: Nifty
      Ink, the collector, the artist’s project, the year 2022 and the artist’s
      own words, each with its sources. The day each Nifty Ink work reached the
      collector, and how, is in the details panel of its page, from the public
      ledger of his wallets.
    </p>
    <p>
      The earlier{' '}
      <a href="https://www.thingelstad.com/art/nnxyz/abstract/">
        Abstract account
      </a>
      also lists scrimshaw.eth as the gift recipient beside #06. The ledger
      confirms the April 2022 gifts; the earlier gallery’s dates and lists are
      the collector’s historical account, kept distinct from a current ownership
      snapshot.
    </p>
    <h2>How the rooms were made</h2>
    <p>
      Every work hangs in one room, in the order it was drawn, and the rooms
      were formed from the images rather than from titles. The galleries were
      curated with AI, working from the artwork, the archived records, the
      ledgers and the artist’s public posts; the text reads feeling and message
      in the work, grounded in the picture and the record, and invents no
      quotation, biography or influence. Comparisons to other artists are the
      gallery’s. Dates are UTC, from the archived creation timestamps.
    </p>
    <p>
      The gallery shows an owned subset. Title sequence does not establish
      creation order, and <a href="/catalogue/">the catalogue</a> lists every
      collection the artist is known to have made, held here or not. Only the
      collector is named; other buyers appear by Ethereum name where they have
      one, and are otherwise counted.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/notes/">Notes from the curator →</a>, on how the rooms, the
      text, the story pages, the audio tour and the map were made.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/technique/">Technique →</a>, how the drawings were built, from
      the recorded order of their strokes.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/map/">See the map of the collection →</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/screensaver/">Run the screen saver →</a>, one work at a time
      with its caption.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/#collections">Explore the collection galleries →</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/">Return to the collection →</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
